
Write ONE invariant function PER PROPERTY (no fixture — it already exists as `Fixture`):

- `pub fn c_<property>(fixture: &mut Fixture) { ... }` — named EXACTLY as the property says, and `pub` —
  runs AFTER EACH fuzzed action of its own campaign. One property = one fn = one build = one fuzz
  run, so each property is measured on its own evidence instead of sharing a budget with its
  neighbours. The fuzzer drives the WHOLE program, so the sequence interleaves other components'
  actions too.
- The name is not yours to choose or derive: each property states it, and it is the same name you
  will pass to `map_checks`. A fn no property names is never built; a property whose fn is missing
  or misspelled fails the build naming it.
- Write the ITEMS ONLY — no file scaffolding. Your code is placed inside a module file the host
  writes, which already opens with that module's `//!` docs and `use super::*;`. Do not write either
  yourself: a `//!` below them is `error[E0753]: expected outer doc comment`, and a second
  `use super::*;` is redundant. Use `//` for any comments of your own.
- Do NOT write `#[invariant_test]` yourself. The host generates the `#[invariant_test]` entry point
  that calls your fn (the macro expands `fn main()` next to itself, so it has to sit at the crate
  root, outside your module).
  Helper items are welcome — `impl Fixture { ... }` blocks, free fns, consts — and because the
  module is gated, a name you choose can never clash with another component's.
- Assert against ON-CHAIN state, not local mirrors:
    let acct = fixture.ctx.read_anchor_account::<SomeState>(&fixture.some_pda).unwrap();
    fuzz_assert_le!(acct.balance, cap, "message");   // fuzz_assert_{eq,ne,lt,le,gt,ge}
- Read RAW lamports / data / owner (bypassing Anchor deserialization) via
  `read_account` / `get_account`, which return `Result<Account>` — unwrap/`?` FIRST,
  THEN the field (there is NO `get_account_lamports`, and `Result` has no `.lamports`):
    let lamports = fixture.ctx.read_account(&fixture.some_pda).unwrap().lamports;
  Existence check: `fixture.ctx.account_exists(&fixture.some_pda)` (returns bool).
- TRANSACTION FEE — do NOT assert an EXACT lamport delta on a signer. The fee payer (the
  FIRST signer of the `action_*`'s `.send()`, e.g. the `depositor`/`authority`) is debited the
  tx fee (~5000 lamports/signature) ON TOP OF whatever lamports the instruction moves, so
  `depositor_before - amount == depositor_after` is WRONG (off by the fee). Assert exact deltas
  on NON-signer accounts (e.g. the vault PDA, which only receives the transfer); for a signer,
  subtract the fee or assert a bound (`<=`) instead of `==`.
- Drive state via the fixture's existing `action_*` methods; do not re-`send()`
  instructions yourself unless necessary.
- A "must be rejected" property is checked HERE, not in the fixture. The fixture's negative actions
  attempt the thing and record the outcome in a field (`self.accepted.<attempt> |= …`); they
  deliberately do not assert, because the fixture is built into every component's fuzz target and an
  assertion there fires in campaigns that never asked about your property. Read the recorded field
  and assert on it, tagged like any other:
      fuzz_assert!(!fixture.accepted.init_without_signer,
          "[authority_must_sign_initialization] init without the authority signing was accepted");
  A recorded flag is the one assertion with no operands to interpolate (see the next bullet) — name
  the attempt that was wrongly accepted instead. If one of your properties is about a rejection and
  the fixture records nothing for it, that is a fixture gap: say so in a `// UNCOVERABLE:` comment
  rather than asserting something vacuous.
- EVERY assertion message MUST do two things: (a) START with its property title in brackets
  (`"[<title>] ..."`) so a counterexample names which property it refutes, and (b) INTERPOLATE THE
  OPERAND VALUES via format args. Passing a custom message makes `fuzz_assert*` use that message
  VERBATIM — the macro's built-in `(left != right)` operand dump is DISCARDED — so a message
  without the values leaves the counterexample untriageable:
      // BAD — says nothing about what the values were, so nobody can tell a real bug from a
      // fixture-modelling mistake without rebuilding and re-running the harness:
      fuzz_assert!(total <= limit, "[deposit_limit_not_exceeded] total supply exceeds limit");
      // GOOD — the message carries the numbers that refute the property:
      fuzz_assert_le!(total, limit,
          "[deposit_limit_not_exceeded] total_supply={} exceeds deposit_limit={}", total, limit);
  Prefer the comparison macros (`fuzz_assert_{eq,ne,lt,le,gt,ge}`) over a bare `fuzz_assert!` so
  the operands are explicit, and when the check LOOPS over accounts include the account key too —
  otherwise the message cannot say which one failed.
- Return ONLY the `pub fn c_<property title>` fns, plus any read helpers they share.

